  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [crazygringo](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=crazygringo) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168578) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168794)\[–\]<br>What? The designers who will be using this to convert PNG's won't have the slightest idea how to access an API. I, as a programmer, don't want to write code to do this either. I think creating a site for this is _awesome_.<br>It would be cool if they created an API as well, and a library (but for what language? so many compatibility issues...), but to say this should "not be a website" is entirely backwards. A website is the most user-friendly and accessible platform possible, and makes perfect sense to be the first step. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [jlarocco](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=jlarocco) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168684) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168578) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168624)\[–\]<br>I have to agree with the OP here. Making this a webpage is silly.<br>If it were a library the program that created the PNG could have saved it in this compressed form in the first place.<br>Choosing the language isn't an issue, either. The obvious choice is C because that's what regular libpng is written in. And every language in existence provides a way to interface with C. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [georgemcbay](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=georgemcbay) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168624) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168578) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168684) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169295)\[–\]<br>"What? The designers who will be using this to convert PNG's won't have the slightest idea how to access an API."<br>For designers it should be a Photoshop plugin (built on the theoretical library) or just a desktop app that can bulk convert entire directories.<br>While dealing with 20 images at a time is certainly nicer than one at a time, a drag & drop website UI like this one is an absolute workflow killer on the design side. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [crazygringo](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=crazygringo) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168652) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168624) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169295)\[–\]<br>Sure, a Photoshop plugin would be great. And a desktop app. And a GIMP plugin. And a Finder extension. And lots of other things too.<br>My point is, a website interface the single most accessible way so that 100% of people can use it, regardless of platform or programming expertise.<br>It is the obvious first choice. That said, of course I hope all the other options come along soon... | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [shawnz](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=shawnz) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169227) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168652) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169113)\[–\]<br>The problem is that websites are the most accessible way to deliver an interface for _humans_. But nobody else can do anything with the tech, and you're limited to the one workflow that the creator had thought of.<br>If this were a library or a command line app like pngcrush, web services could be spawned from it in days, easily. Or desktop apps. Or editor plugins. But in the current format, it's inextensible.<br>(disclaimer: this isn't always true, but it's true in this case) | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [ars](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=ars) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169113) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168652) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169227) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169295)\[–\]<br>GIMP already has an interface to reduce color depth, but it doesn't support transparency. (Maybe 2.8 does, haven't tried it yet.)<br>So this should not be a plugin for gimp, but rather a bug fix.<br>It would also be nice if gimp could open images with indexed transparency. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [shawnz](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=shawnz) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169252) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169113) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169295)\[–\]<br>You're right, proper transparency support is step 1 -- but that isn't to say that GIMP couldn't have some sort of automatic lossy compression tool (akin to this) on _top_ of that tech.<br>EDIT: I suppose this software is equivalent to gimp's "Automatically select palette" option when converting images to indexed. Perhaps that option could be replaced with a list of algorithms to choose from, like how the Size dialog lets you choose your own scaling algorithms. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [ars](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=ars) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169584) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169252) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169295)\[–\]<br>Yes, exactly. This tool is simply because "Automatically select palette" doesn't support transparency. If it did there would be no need for this.<br>I'm not sure you really need multiple algorithms, the algorithm itself isn't anything special as far as I know - it just supports transparency that's all.<br>You can get somewhat similar results by selecting all the transparent and partially transparent parts and saving the selection. Then flatten the image (i.e. mix the transparency into the background color), reduce the color depth, then use the color to alpha option only on the selection (not the whole image) to subtract the color and bring back the transparency.<br>Then count how many colors you have, if you have too many, undo everything, and choose a lower number for color depth and try again. It helps to choose a color that does not otherwise exist in the image for the background mixing color. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [njharman](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=njharman) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169295) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168578) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168624) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168794)\[–\]<br>\> A website is the most user-friendly and accessible platform possible<br>A website requires a browser, a human, and bunch of clicking around. Every single time you want to do this.<br>API / library requires script to be written and added to production pipeline. Once. Then runs without human intervention.<br>The most human friendly interface is the one that saves humans from doing boring, tedious, automatable tasks. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [pornel](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=pornel) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168794) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168578) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168957)\[–\]<br>I believe this website uses pngquant to perform the optimization. You can get the tool from [http://pngquant.org](http://pngquant.org/) (it's open-source and pretty easy to use as a library too). | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [mrjd](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=mrjd) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169496) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168794) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168957)\[–\]<br>You're right, it's listed on pngquant's site too as one of the GUI's. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [nostromo](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=nostromo) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168957) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168794) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169544)\[–\]<br>"Write programs that do one thing and do it well."<br>also: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worse\_is\_better](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Worse_is_better) | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [kamakazizuru](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=kamakazizuru) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169544) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168957) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168451)\[–\]<br>I guess they'll take a freemium approach with time - offer the API and so on as paid models that people can pay for. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [molf](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=molf) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168451) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168419) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168269)\[–\]<br>Thanks for all the feedback, everyone! We're still working on making TinyPNG easier to use and removing some of the kinks. Keep the comments coming!<br>We created this service because we (our web agency) were building a couple of sites that used very large transparent images. Unfortunately the file sizes were also massive, so we went looking for compression mechanisms beyond traditional lossless optimisation tools, which simply did not reduce our files enough. We built TinyPNG around a couple of existing open source quantising and compression tools.<br>Initially it was an internal tool, but we were extremely surprised by the consistent good quality of the results. So we decided to share it as an online service so that it is as easy as possible for everyone to reduce file sizes. An API will be coming soon!<br>Is it always a good idea to use lossy compression? No, certainly not. There are some edge cases that perform poorly. But we think the results are impressive. Use your own judgment! :-)<br>We actually went looking for PNG files on very popular sites (Facebook, Google, Github, Duckduckgo, many others) and almost all of them could benefit from TinyPNG's file size reduction without noticeable quality loss. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [willvarfar](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=willvarfar) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169016) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168451) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168269)\[–\]<br>_which_ existing open-source tools? | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [molf](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=molf) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169050) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168451) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169016) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168269)\[–\]<br>Mostly pngquant (the new version), optipng and advpng. We are still tuning parameters and swapping in and out tools based on our benchmarks and testing suite. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [simonsarris](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=simonsarris) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168269) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168451) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168259)\[–\]<br>Wow it took my png from 659 bytes to 681 and lets me download the 681 byte one. :(<br>So I took a PNG from wikipedia:<br>221.0kb (its actually 215 KB or a little more than 220,000 bytes) to a claimed 47.8 KB (really 46.7 KB). Nice!<br>Running PNGGauntlet, a lossless compression app, with the same file compressed nothing. Wise wikipedians must have already done that.<br>But the results from tinypng look _awful_. Here you can flip between the original and the compressed:<br>[http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/PNG\_trans...](http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/47/PNG_transparency_demonstration_1.png)<br>[http://i.imgur.com/QOLo3.png](http://i.imgur.com/QOLo3.png) | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [bosch](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=bosch) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168400) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168269) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169141)\[–\]<br>I wouldn't say they look awful, there's a difference in the background part of the image but the main focus has little change.<br>It's a trade-off, but I would imagine that if there's thousands of files it could be a significant savings on bandwidth, and you could always keep the original for download but not display puroses. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [mceachen](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=mceachen) [on June 28, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4170219) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168269) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168400) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169141)\[–\]<br>There's very clear posterization in the yellow die and in the green die's highlights.<br>That shouldn't be surprising -- high color depth images need more than 256 colors to represent their image with fidelity.<br>See more: [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posterization](http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Posterization) | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [ars](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=ars) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169141) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168269) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168400) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168697)\[–\]<br>Here are the results of pngnq which does exactly the same thing as this tool. It created a file size of 46K, (which I was then able to reduce to 41K with pngout followed by advpng).<br>[http://i.imgur.com/VPc2k.png](http://i.imgur.com/VPc2k.png)<br>[http://pngnq.sourceforge.net/](http://pngnq.sourceforge.net/)<br>256 colors may simply not be enough for this image.<br>(Incidentally I was able to slightly reduce your image to 46K with pngout.) | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [projct](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=projct) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168697) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168269) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4169141) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168471)\[–\]<br>Riot\[1\] (windows) does as good or better than pnggauntlet and lets you preview things. supports the same backends I think (pngout, optipng, advpng, etc) and some extra dithering options... perhaps worth a look, I switched from pnggauntlet. :)<br>\[1\] [http://luci.criosweb.ro/riot/](http://luci.criosweb.ro/riot/) | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [fluxon](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=fluxon) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168471) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168269) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168697) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168259)\[–\]<br>There are \_slight\_ artifacts on the defocused green die. At typical Wikipedia in-article display sizes (400x300, in this case), this is acceptable. Viewing in Win Firefox 13.01, Windows gamma. I'd use tinypng on large files before downscaling for web use. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [ars](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=ars) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169142) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168269) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168471) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168259)\[–\]<br>Use it after downscaling not before.<br>If you have a good downscaling tool it will create a larger number of intermediate colors when you downscale and you'll actually end up with a larger image! | |
