  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [trebor](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=trebor) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168114) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168100) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168177) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168796)\[–\]<br>Ditto. I'd like to see some side-by-side comparisons with PNGOUT, PNGCrush, OptiPNG, and AdvPNG. I use ImageOptim on my Mac to automate my PNG optimization—and it can get some impressive gains. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [kizza](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=kizza) [on June 28, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4170665) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168100) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168114) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168692)\[–\]<br>On the Mac I use ImageAlpha to reduce the colours to 256 (it has a preview window so you can see if it's OK), then when saving the file I choose the option to send it to ImageOptim.<br>On Linux you can get the same by using pngquant, then using trimage. AFAIK there's no GUI frontend for pngquant so I guess how many colours would be ok and check before running trimage.<br>The reason why I use 2 tools is that ImageAlpha/pngquant is lossy and ImageOptim/trimage is lossless. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [molf](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=molf) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168692) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168100) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168114) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4170665) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168291)\[–\]<br>The tools you mention use lossless compression. For 24-bit PNG files that means they probably won't come close the kind of lossy compression used by TinyPNG. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [trebor](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=trebor) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4169487) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168100) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168692) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168291)\[–\]<br>The comparison will be subjective. One will retain full detail (24-bit color) and the other will not. The "better" will be subjective, as I said.<br>I wish Photoshop gave the option to save an 8-bit PNG with a FULL alpha channel—that's what this seems to do. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [eli](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=eli) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168291) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168100) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168114) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168692) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168796)\[–\]<br>I think ImageOptim uses the pngcrush library.<br>This website looks neat, but it seems like a CLI that can become part of my automated workflow would generally be more useful. | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [cs702](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=cs702) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168796) \| [prev](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168100) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168945)\[–\]<br>Can't you achieve the same results with ImageMagick? For example, \`mogrify -format png8 \*.png\` converts all png images in the current directory to indexed versions.<br>Moreover, ImageMagick itself recommends a variety of more advanced lossless and lossy png compression utilites: [http://www.imagemagick.org/Usage/formats/#png\_non-im](http://www.imagemagick.org/Usage/formats/#png_non-im)<br>Then I opened the original panda PNG with GIMP, clicked on Image -> Mode -> Indexed -> Optimized palete -> 255 colors, and obtained an even smaller file size.<br>What's so special about this? | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [pornel](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=pornel) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168830) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168796) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168945)\[–\]<br>You can generate the same image format with ImageMagick, Fireworks, pngnq and pngquant (the last one seems to be powering the site).<br>The difference is in quality of quantization. The poorer the algorithm, the more banding artifacts or dithering noise you'll get.<br>Usually less optimal quantization also gives worse quality/filesize ratio (since palette entries are "spent" on less noticeable areas of the image). | |
  | |     |     |     |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | ![](https://news.ycombinator.com/s.gif) |  | [cs702](https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=cs702) [on June 27, 2012](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4168859) \| [root](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168796) \| [parent](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168830) \| [next](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=4167964#4168945)\[–\]<br>_You can generate the same image format with ImageMagick, Fireworks, pngnq and pngquant (the last one seems to be powering the site)_ <br>Exactly: I don't see what's _special_ about tinyPNG. | |
